    subheadline: "Rising Treasury yields and AI‑market jitters keep investors cautious, but US indices finish higher.",
    keyHighlights: ["U.S. Treasury yields climb, pressuring equities", "Nasdaq gains for fourth straight week", "Jim Cramer highlights Starbucks buy level amid AI concerns", "Bond market nerves ease temporarily", "Global indices rebound but sentiment remains weak"],
    executiveSummary: "Bond yields have risen sharply, tightening the funding environment for corporations and weighing on equity valuations. Despite this, U.S. major indices closed higher on Friday, with the Nasdaq posting its fourth consecutive weekly gain, buoyed by a modest rebound in technology stocks and a cautious optimism about the upcoming iPhone Duo pre‑orders. Jim Cramer, during CNBC’s Investing Club, flagged a new buy level for Starbucks while cautioning that the broader market remains fragile due to rising inflation expectations and AI‑related volatility. The Treasury curve has flattened, indicating market expectations of a more moderate rate path ahead. Quick Answer: Bond yields are climbing, pressuring equities, but U.S. indices managed modest gains amid AI concerns.",
    marketBackground: "The global bond market has entered a phase of heightened volatility as the Federal Reserve’s policy stance signals further tightening. Rising 10‑year and 30‑year Treasury yields have reached multi‑year highs, squeezing corporate borrowing costs and dampening investor appetite for risk‑tolerant assets. In parallel, the U.S. equity market has been navigating a complex landscape: AI‑driven valuation adjustments, slowing consumer sentiment, and persistent inflationary pressures. Yet, the technology sector, led by firms like Apple and Nvidia, has shown resilience, contributing to the Nasdaq’s steady climb.",
    detailedAnalysis: "## Bullish Factors\\n- Technology earnings beat expectations, supporting Nasdaq gains\\n- Anticipated iPhone Duo pre‑orders expected to lift Apple’s revenue outlook\\n- Fed’s forward guidance suggests a potential pause in rate hikes, easing bond market nerves\\n\\n## Bearish Factors\\n- Rising Treasury yields increase discount rates, compressing equity valuations\\n- Persistent inflation expectations raise concerns over future corporate profitability\\n- AI‑market jitters and concerns over revenue projections for AI‑focused stocks\\n\\n## Risk Analysis\\n- Yield spikes could trigger a broader equity sell‑off if rates rise faster than expected\\n- Consumer sentiment may deteriorate if inflation continues to outpace wage growth\\n- Market overreliance on technology may expose investors to sector‑specific risks\\n\\n## Expert Perspective\\nJim Cramer, host of CNBC’s Investing Club, highlighted Starbucks as a potential buy, citing a favorable technical setup and a solid earnings outlook. He warned that the market remains “not that good” overall, citing rising bond yields and flat oil prices as key headwinds.\\n\\n## Historical Comparison\\n- The current yield environment mirrors the 2018‑2019 period when Fed tightening led to a 5‑year high in the 10‑year yield and a 10‑year decline in the S&P 500.\\n- Unlike that era, however, the current equity rebound is supported by a stronger technology sector and more optimistic corporate earnings forecasts.\\n\\n## Market Impact\\n- The flattening of the Treasury curve suggests market participants expect a moderation in rate hikes, providing some relief to equities.\\n- Nonetheless, the persistent rise in yields continues to weigh on corporate balance sheets and could dampen future growth prospects.",
    expertInsights: "Jim Cramer flagged Starbucks’ new buy level at $115, citing a strong earnings trajectory and a favorable technical trend. He cautioned that while the tech sector remains resilient, the broader market is still sensitive to rising bond yields and AI‑market volatility. Analysts note that the flattening Treasury curve may signal a pause in Fed tightening, providing a potential lift for equities.",
